{
  "answer": "The retained passages support a distinction between cryptocurrency being property capable of trust and a particular customer having a beneficial interest. The 2023 conclusion denied the claimed trusts for customers governed by the 2018 terms, preserving the non-consenting-customer exception. The 2025 passages recognise pooled beneficial interests for non-consenting customers, distinguish other party relationships, and make consent classification depend on transaction and instruction evidence. They do not establish a general trust for token holders or a test for fulfilling a Product Provider’s responsibilities under 26EC22. Transfer requires a justified legal analogy, applicable regulatory standards and actual product-specific facts. Scenario seven and complete subsequent treatment remain unresolved.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "inference": "Text: capacity to be trust property does not establish that a trust was created. The conclusion turns on applicable terms, objective treatment of assets and intention, with an express customer exception.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The complete reasons and relevant terms needed to determine the precise scope of the holding.",
        "Evidence establishing which terms governed any particular customer."
      ],
      "proposition": "The 2023 judgment treats cryptocurrency as property capable of trust but rejects the claimed trust for customers subject to the 2018 terms, preserving the non-consenting exception.",
      "status": "Supported by retained conclusion; scope beyond the passages not checked",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Except the Non-Consenting Customers, the 2018 T&C applied to, and governed the relationship between all the Customers (be it Group A, B or C customers) and Gatecoin",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.conclusion-82"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Cryptocurrency is “property” and is capable of being held on trust",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.conclusion-82"
        },
        {
          "quote": "However, the terms of the 2018 T&C show that there was no certainty of intention to create a trust over the cryptocurrencies held by Gatecoin",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.conclusion-82"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: the judgment quotes principles from Re Lehman Brothers. Deduction: labels such as custody or ownership cannot alone establish a proprietary interest; the specific agreement and relationship require objective assessment.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The relevant agreements, governing law and actual dealings for each party relationship.",
        "Full reasons showing the application of the quoted principles."
      ],
      "proposition": "Ownership and trust conclusions must remain specific to the parties and their relationship.",
      "status": "Supported as quoted reasoning, not an independently verified universal rule",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "The words used by the parties such as ‘trust’, ‘custody’, ‘belonging’, ‘ownership’, ‘title’, may be persuasive, but they are not conclusive in favour of the recognition of B’s proprietary interest in the property, if the terms of the agreement or relationship, viewed objectively, compel a different conclusion.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.terms-and-intention"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: paragraph 8 differentiates transaction scenarios and permits contrary evidence. Deduction: an account entry after the relevant date does not by itself establish customer consent; platform access and instruction timing matter.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The complete definition of non-consenting customers and the relevant consent mechanism.",
        "Customer-specific evidence of platform use, instruction timing and transaction type."
      ],
      "proposition": "The 2025 classification is evidence-sensitive: scenarios one to four presumptively preserve non-consenting status; scenarios five and six prima facie exclude it but permit proof of qualifying instructions.",
      "status": "Supported by retained paragraph 8, subject to the unresolved annexure and disposition reconciliation",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "The customers should be regarded as NCCs unless there is evidence to show that they have accessed or used the Platform to effect the transactions",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.classification-8"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The transactions described in the 5th to 6th scenarios on their face required the customers to access or use the Platform to effect them and, prima facie, the customers fall outside the definition of NCC. However, if the customers are able to prove that they gave the instruction to Gatecoin before the Relevant Date or without using the Platform, they should be regarded as NCCs.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.classification-8"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: the 2025 passages distinguish NCC beneficial ownership, BlueFire account assets, ETD breach-of-trust claims and Samara’s unsecured claim. Deduction: these outcomes cannot be collapsed into a single rule about exchange customers or token holders.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The full facts and reasoning supporting each distinct relationship.",
        "Evidence linking an assessed claimant to the relevant class and property pool."
      ],
      "proposition": "The 2025 judgment recognises different proprietary and personal rights for distinct parties.",
      "status": "Supported by retained summary and disposition",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Each NCC is a beneficial tenant-in-common in a pool of cryptocurrency and fiat currency.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The Currencies recorded in BlueFire’s accounts are assets of Gatecoin.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.earlier-decision"
        },
        {
          "quote": "ETD represents a cause of action by ETD Holder against Gatecoin for breach of trust.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Gatecoin does not hold the Subject Bitcoins or the Specified Account on trust for Mr Samara. The Specified Account is not a NCC and, therefore, Mr Samara is only an unsecured creditor to the extent of his claim less the amount he already received through execution of the HCA Judgment.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text tension: paragraph 8 rejects scenario seven’s factual existence, whereas paragraph 95 includes it within a rebuttable exclusion. Judgment: retain both statements; the supplied passages do not justify choosing one or constructing a complete automatic classifier.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Reconciliation of paragraph 8, paragraph 95 and the annexure against the complete reasons and operative order.",
        "Any authoritative correction or clarification."
      ],
      "proposition": "Scenario seven remains unresolved, and the annexure introduces additional classification and evidence requirements.",
      "status": "OPEN_SOURCE_INTERPRETATION",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "The transactions described in the 7th scenario did not exist and the customers should not be regarded as NCC.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.classification-8"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The 5th to 7th scenarios prima facie fall outside the definition of NCC but if the customers can prove that they gave the relevant instructions to Gatecoin before the Relevant Date or without using the Platform, they should be regarded as NCCs.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Any Customer who claims to have transactions enumerated in paragraphs 1.2(b) shall notify the Liquidators and provide evidence to the Liquidators in support of such a claim within 21 days from the date hereof.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.annexure-issue1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Deduction: Gatecoin may inform analysis of underlying ownership relationships, but regulatory responsibility and trust ownership answer different questions. Applying either proposition requires an explicit legal bridge. Paragraph 10 allocates continuing responsibility; neither its applicability nor a Gatecoin analogy establishes fulfillment.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Applicability of the retained 26EC22 edition to the entity, product, activity and assessment interval, including conditions and amendments.",
        "Proof that the assessed entity is the relevant Product Provider.",
        "A reasoned comparison of Gatecoin’s exchange-customer relationships with the product’s issuer, trustee, custodian, register keeper and investors.",
        "Product terms, consent evidence, governing law, title structure and the legal effect of on-chain and off-chain records.",
        "An applicable standard for adequate management, operational soundness and proper ownership records, together with evidence of actual performance."
      ],
      "proposition": "The retained judgments do not themselves establish a Product Provider’s ownership structure or fulfillment of paragraph 10 and paragraph 11.",
      "status": "Source-grounded deduction; transfer and fulfillment unestablished",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u41.3664"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: the later judgment expressly records the earlier conclusions. Judgment: this supports continuity in the retained proceedings, but does not establish complete subsequent treatment or independently verify the candidate’s broader treatment label.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Complete later judicial treatment, including any appeals, distinctions or qualifications.",
        "Inspection of the full judgments before making broader precedential claims."
      ],
      "proposition": "The retained 2025 judgment records the 2023 conclusions; complete later treatment remains unknown.",
      "status": "Limited support; subsequent treatment unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "In summary, it was held that: Cryptocurrency is property and is capable of being held on trust; With the exception of the Non-consenting Customers (“NCCs”), the 2018 T&C applied to, and governed the relationship between all the Customers (be it Group A, B or C) and Gatecoin.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.earlier-decision"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "legal_correctness_established": false,
  "question_id": "gatecoin",
  "rival_readings": [
    "Scenario seven: paragraph 8 treats the described transactions as nonexistent and excludes NCC status; paragraph 95 includes scenario seven among cases permitting proof of qualifying instructions. Neither reading is conclusively harmonised with the annexure.",
    "Transfer: Gatecoin can inform an objective assessment of product ownership and trust intention where materially comparable facts and law are established. It cannot, without further premises, supply a regulatory discharge test under 26EC22."
  ],
  "unresolved": [
    "Full reasons, operative orders and rendered source fidelity have not been checked here; candidate assertions of full review are not independent verification.",
    "The candidate case manifests give the same source hash for both judgments, while the supplied source-file records give different hashes; provenance reconciliation remains outstanding.",
    "Complete subsequent treatment and the precise precedential scope remain unverified.",
    "The scenario-seven tension and annexure reconciliation remain open.",
    "No actual Product Provider facts or applicable fulfillment standard have been supplied. Formal rule or solver acceptance would not establish legal truth."
  ]
